{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.QDifferenceGaugeMatrix","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.QDifferenceGaugeMatrix","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.NonsingularInverse"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QDifferenceGaugeMatrix.det_charMatrix_conj","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QDifferenceGaugeMatrix.det_charMatrix_conj","kind":"theorem","line":106,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QDifferenceGaugeMatrix.lean#L106-L120","doc":"**The contrast: conjugation leaves the characteristic polynomial alone.**\n\n`det(X·1 − F A F⁻¹) = det(X·1 − A)` for invertible `F`. This is why the Seiberg–Witten\nspectral curve of a *Higgs field* is well defined — gauge acts there by conjugation — and\nits failure for the twisted q-difference action `(σ_q F)⁻¹ A F` is precisely the finding\nof #7208. The probe checks this numerically over `ℚ` (T3); this is the statement it is a\nsample of. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QDifferenceGaugeMatrix.det_charMatrix_smul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QDifferenceGaugeMatrix.det_charMatrix_smul","kind":"theorem","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QDifferenceGaugeMatrix.lean#L88-L104","doc":"**Scaling the system matrix moves the characteristic polynomial by a substitution.**\n\n`det(X·1 − t·A) = t^n · det((X/t)·1 − A)` for `t ≠ 0`, where `n` is the size. So the\nspectral locus of `t·A` is that of `A` pulled back along `X ↦ X/t` — it moves, unless the\nlocus happens to be invariant under that substitution.\n\nCombined with `scalar_gauge_eq`, this is the dictionary #7208 assumed: *a gauge\ntransformation of the q-difference system* and *a change of the `X` coordinate over the\nbase* are the same move. `QGaugeSpectralRigidity.weight_relations` then measures what\nsurviving it would cost a monic cubic — every non-leading coefficient. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.QDifferenceGaugeMatrix.scalar_gauge_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.QDifferenceGaugeMatrix.scalar_gauge_eq","kind":"theorem","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/QDifferenceGaugeMatrix.lean#L70-L86","doc":"**The q-difference gauge law for a scalar gauge** — DVRSZ eq. (2.4.1),\n`B = (σ_q F)⁻¹ A F`, with `F = c·1` and cleared of the inverse.\n\nRead `s = σ_q(c)` and `t = c`: the hypothesis is `(σ_q F) · B = A · F`, and the\nconclusion is `B = (c / σ_q c) · A`. Taking `c = z^k` and `σ_q : z ↦ q·z` gives\n`c / σ_q c = q^{-k}`, so a monomial gauge multiplies the system matrix by a power of `q`\n— which is the step #7208 needs and cites rather than proves.\n\nThe action is **not** conjugation: the two sides carry *different* scalars, `σ_q c` on\nthe left and `c` on the right, and they fail to cancel exactly when `σ_q c ≠ c`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}